        "question": {
          ":question/id": "752e1a2a-e67c-5beb-8772-e724c0273fbe",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)= (y - 4)\\ln (x - 1),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}((y - 4)\\ln (x - 1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y - 4}{x - 1} \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}((y - 4)\\ln (x - 1)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x - 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y - 4}{x - 1} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y - 4}{x - 1} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y - 4}{x - 1}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x - 1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y - 4=0,$ which means $y=4.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x - 1)=0,$ which means that $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(2,4).$",
          ":question/answer-fields": [
