        "question": {
          ":question/id": "3647783a-5d52-54ad-9327-b4f3e8b871ba",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Given that $g(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+y\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y+z\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-4x-4y-2z$, find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\u2207g(x,y,z)=0.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202g}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+y\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y+z\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-4x-4y-2z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+0+0-4-0-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+1-4 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x-3 \\\\ \\frac{\u2202g}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+y\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y+z\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-4x-4y-2z) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y+y\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}+0-0-4-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y+1-4 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y-3 \\\\ \\frac{\u2202g}{\u2202z} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+y\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y+z\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-4x-4y-2z) \\\\  & =0+0+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z+z\\cdot \\frac{1}{z}-0-0-2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z+1-2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\u2207g(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x-3)\\,i+(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y-3)\\,j+(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-1)\\,k.$ \n\nNow, we solve $\u2207g(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x-3)\\,i+(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y-3)\\,j+(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-1)\\,k & =0\\text{\u00a0}i+0\\text{\u00a0}j+0\\text{\u00a0}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x-3=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061y-3=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=e^{3}.$  Similarly, from the second and third equations, we obtain $y=e^{3}$ and $z=e.$\n\nHence, $\u2207g(x,y,z)=0$ at the point $(e^{3},e^{3},e).$",
