        "question": {
          ":question/id": "83cf6056-6c47-5ff9-8e15-78cbf544f7cd",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Given $h(x,y,z)=3xy+3x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\u2207h(x,y,z)=0.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(3xy+3x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =3y+3{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =3y+3{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(3xy+3x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =3x+6xy+0 \\\\  & =3x+6xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(3xy+3x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\u2207h(x,y,z)=(3y+3{y}^{2})\\,i+(3x+6xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $\u2207h(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y+3{y}^{2})\\,i+(3x+6xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k & =0\\text{\u00a0}i+0\\text{\u00a0}j+0\\text{\u00a0}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3y+3{y}^{2}=0 \\\\ 3x+6xy=0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation can be written as $y(3+3y)=0$, which gives $y=0$ or $y=-1.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+6x(0) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+6x(-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, $\u2207h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-1,0).$",
