          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
          ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+5)+5(y-7)^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\u2207f(x,y)=0.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+5)+5(y-7)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+5}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5} \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+5)+5(y-7)^{2}) \\\\  & =0+5\\cdot 2(y-7) \\\\  & =10(y-7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\u2207f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,i+10(y-7)\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $\u2207f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,i+10(y-7)\\,j=0\\text{\u00a0}i+0\\text{\u00a0}j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}=0 \\\\ 10(y-7)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ and from the second equation, we obtain $y=7.$ \n\nTherefore, $\u2207f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(0,7).$",
