          "title": "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "1e2ee867-d428-5555-91ee-f11ad262b673",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
          ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-10x\\operatorname{cos}\u20613y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\frac{\\pi}{3} ,$ where $\u2207f(x,y)=0.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({x}^{2}-10x\\operatorname{cos}\u20613y) \\\\  & =2x-10\\operatorname{cos}\u20613y \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({x}^{2}-10x\\operatorname{cos}\u20613y) \\\\  & =30x\\operatorname{sin}\u20613y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\u2207f(x,y)=2(x-5\\operatorname{cos}\u20613y)\\,i+30x\\operatorname{sin}\u20613y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $\u2207f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-5\\operatorname{cos}\u20613y)\\,i+30x\\operatorname{sin}\u20613y\\,j=0\\text{\u00a0}i+0\\text{\u00a0}j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2(x-5\\operatorname{cos}\u20613y)=0 \\\\ 30x\\operatorname{sin}\u20613y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=5\\operatorname{cos}\u20613y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}150\\operatorname{cos}\u20613y\\operatorname{sin}\u20613y & =0 \\\\ 75\\operatorname{sin}\u20616y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=5\\operatorname{cos}\u20610=5.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get $x=5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, $\u2207f(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\frac{\\pi}{3} ,$ at two points: $(5,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$",
